<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tokugawa Yoshimune</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1684">1684</a>–<a href="/years/1751">1751</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tokugawa Yoshimune (1684–1751) was lord of the Kii domain from 1705 and became the eighth Tokugawa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a> in 1716, the first from a branch line. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoho-reforms">Kyōhō reforms</a> cut spending, raised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> revenue, introduced a petition box and relaxed the ban on Western books. He retired in 1745.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tokugawa-yoshimune.jpg" alt="Tokugawa Yoshimune" width="320" height="312" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kanō Tadanobu · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tokugawa_Yoshimune.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Yoshimune was born on 27 November 1684 in Kii Province, ruled by his father Tokugawa Mitsusada, and was a great-grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a>. He was not the son of any shōgun. Ieyasu had been aware that his direct line might die out and selected three sons to found the gosanke, the Owari, Kii and Mito houses, which would supply a shōgun if there were no male heir. Yoshimune belonged to the Kii house, founded by Ieyasu's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-yorinobu">Tokugawa Yorinobu</a>.</p><p>His childhood name was Genroku, and in 1697 he took the name Shinnosuke. In 1705 his father and two older brothers died, and the shōgun Tokugawa Tsunayoshi appointed him daimyō of Kii, when he was 21. He took the name Yorikata. Kii was rich, with over 500,000 koku, but owed large debts to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a>, and a tsunami in 1707 destroyed coastal areas and killed many people. He did not trust conservative Confucian advisers such as Arai Hakuseki and worked to stabilise the domain. The boy-shōgun Tokugawa Ietsugu died in early 1716 aged seven and without an heir, and the bakufu chose the next shōgun from a cadet line.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Yoshimune became shōgun in 1716 and served for thirty years. He dismissed Arai Hakuseki and began the Kyōhō Reforms, known above all for their financial measures. He established the gosankyō, three houses that did not rule domains and were founded by two of his sons and a son of his successor Ieshige. Some later shōguns came from the Hitotsubashi line. He also tried to revive swordsmithing, and in 1721 held a contest of smiths from the daimyō <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a>, and he ordered the compilation of the Kyōhō Meibutsu Chō, a list of the best swords in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>.</p><p>Foreign books had been strictly banned since 1640, but in 1720 Yoshimune relaxed the rule. This began an influx of foreign books and translations and the development of Western studies, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rangaku">rangaku</a>. The change may have been influenced by lectures given before him by the astronomer and philosopher Nishikawa Joken.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1745 Yoshimune retired, took the title Ōgosho, as Ieyasu and Hidetada had done on retirement, and handed his public post to his eldest son. He died on 12 July 1751 and was buried at Kan'ei-ji. He is considered among the best of the Tokugawa shōguns. He is known for repealing the ban on Western literature.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tokugawa Yoshimune” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When the main line of shoguns ended in 1716, the Kii lord <b>Tokugawa Yoshimune</b> became the eighth shogun and launched the Kyoho reforms.</blockquote><a href="/state/kii-domain">Kii Domain</a></li><li><blockquote>Tokugawa Muneharu defied Shogun <b>Yoshimune</b>'s austerity by encouraging theatre, festivals and commerce, making Nagoya thrive, until he was forced to retire in 1739.</blockquote><a href="/state/owari-domain">Owari Domain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Wakayama, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.23,135.17&amp;year=1727" aria-label="Wakayama on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7990 923 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Tokugawa Yoshimune"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8440 1193h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8440 1193h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.23,135.17&amp;year=1727">What was here in 1727? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
